{
  "vertical-premium": {
    "title": "The Vertical Premium: Height and Housing Prices",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How does the <strong>relative height</strong> of a property from flood elevation affect housing prices? This study examines the <em>\"vertical premium\"</em> - the additional value homebuyers place on properties situated higher above potential flood levels.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Properties located higher above flood elevation command significant price premiums of approximately <strong>2-3% per meter</strong>, even after controlling for other factors. This effect amplified dramatically after the 2013 Calgary flood.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis: Vertical Premium Effect</h3><div class='chart-grid'><div class='chart-container'><h4>Property Value vs. Height Above Flood Elevation</h4><canvas id='concept-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Scatter plot showing the positive relationship between property elevation and market value. The trend line demonstrates a consistent 2-3% increase per meter.</p></div><div class='chart-container'><h4>Pre-Flood vs. Post-Flood Price Premium</h4><canvas id='premium-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Comparison of price premiums before and after the 2013 flood event. Notice the significant amplification in premium effect post-flood, especially for properties 6m+ above flood elevation.</p></div></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: 2013 Calgary Flood</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>$6B+</div><div class='stat-label'>Total Damages</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>100,000+</div><div class='stat-label'>Affected Residents</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>5 days</div><div class='stat-label'>Peak Flood Duration</div></div></div><p>The research analyzes one of Canada's costliest natural disasters, which provides a unique natural experiment to understand how actual flood experience influences housing market behavior.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Calgary residential property transactions combined with detailed flood elevation maps</li><li><strong>Analysis Period:</strong> Pre-flood (2010-2013) and post-flood (2013-2016) comparison</li><li><strong>Key Variable:</strong> Vertical distance (meters) from property to flood elevation line</li><li><strong>Statistical Approach:</strong> Hedonic pricing model with spatial controls and temporal comparison</li><li><strong>Sample Size:</strong> Over 50,000 residential property transactions</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Vertical Premium Exists:</strong> Each additional meter above flood elevation adds approximately 2-3% to property value, controlling for other property characteristics</li><li><strong>Post-Flood Amplification:</strong> The premium increased significantly after the 2013 flood event, with properties 10m+ above flood elevation showing nearly 19% premium</li><li><strong>Spatial Variation:</strong> Effects are strongest in areas with direct flood experience and diminish with distance from flooded areas</li><li><strong>Market Capitalization:</strong> The housing market rapidly incorporated flood risk information, with premiums appearing within months of the disaster</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfd9\ufe0f For Urban Planning</h4><ul><li>Prioritize flood mitigation investments in high-value areas</li><li>Inform zoning and development policies in flood-prone regions</li><li>Design equitable disaster recovery strategies</li><li>Better understand market-based risk valuation</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfe1 For Homeowners & Investors</h4><ul><li>Quantify flood risk impact on property values</li><li>Make informed home purchase decisions</li><li>Evaluate elevation-based protection investments</li><li>Develop better insurance strategies</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>This research introduces the concept of <em>\"vertical premium\"</em> as a distinct component of housing value, separate from traditional flood plain designations. Unlike binary flood zone classifications (in/out), it captures the <strong>continuous relationship</strong> between elevation and value, providing a more nuanced understanding of flood risk capitalization in housing markets.</p></div>"
  },
  "mental-health-covid": {
    "title": "Mental Health and COVID-19: The Role of Walkable Built Environment",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How did the COVID-19 pandemic affect mental health facility visits, and what role did the <strong>walkable built environment</strong> play in supporting mental health during this crisis?</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Neighborhoods with <strong>better walkability</strong> showed smaller declines in mental health facility visits during COVID-19. Access to walkable environments acted as a <strong>protective factor</strong> for mental health during the pandemic.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Mental Health Facility Visits During COVID-19</h4><canvas id='mental-health-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Line chart showing how mental health facility visits changed during COVID-19 in areas with high vs low walkability. High walkability areas maintained better mental health service access.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: COVID-19 Mental Health Crisis</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>43%</div><div class='stat-label'>Increase in Anxiety/Depression</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>2020-2021</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Period</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Atlanta, GA</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div></div><p>The COVID-19 pandemic led to unprecedented levels of mental health challenges, with anxiety and depression rates soaring globally. This study examines how built environment features influenced mental health service utilization during this critical period.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Mental health facility visit records linked with built environment measurements</li><li><strong>Walkability Measures:</strong> Street connectivity, pedestrian infrastructure, mixed land use, and access to amenities</li><li><strong>Analysis Period:</strong> Before and during COVID-19 pandemic (2019-2021)</li><li><strong>Statistical Approach:</strong> Difference-in-differences analysis controlling for socioeconomic factors</li><li><strong>Sample:</strong> Metropolitan Atlanta area neighborhoods</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Walkability as Protective Factor:</strong> Neighborhoods with higher walkability scores experienced smaller decreases in mental health facility visits during the pandemic</li><li><strong>Access to Nature:</strong> Proximity to parks and green spaces was particularly important during lockdown periods</li><li><strong>Pedestrian Infrastructure:</strong> Presence of sidewalks and safe walking routes correlated with maintained mental health service access</li><li><strong>Equity Implications:</strong> The protective effect of walkability was stronger in lower-income neighborhoods, highlighting environmental justice dimensions</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfe5 For Public Health</h4><ul><li>Integrate built environment planning into mental health strategies</li><li>Prioritize walkability improvements in pandemic preparedness</li><li>Recognize outdoor spaces as critical health infrastructure</li><li>Design interventions that leverage environmental factors</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfd9\ufe0f For Urban Planning</h4><ul><li>Invest in walkable infrastructure as health resilience strategy</li><li>Ensure equitable distribution of walkable neighborhoods</li><li>Maintain pedestrian access during public health emergencies</li><li>Integrate health outcomes into walkability planning</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>This research is among the first to quantitatively demonstrate the <strong>mental health benefits of walkable environments during a pandemic</strong>. It provides evidence that urban design decisions have direct implications for population mental health resilience in times of crisis.</p></div>"
  },
  "urban-trees-safety": {
    "title": "Urban Trees and Neighborhood Safety: The Role of Upkeep",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Do urban trees make neighborhoods feel safer or more dangerous? This study examines the <strong>nuanced relationship</strong> between street trees and perceived safety, revealing that <em>neighborhood upkeep matters more than tree presence alone</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Trees <strong>increase perceived safety</strong> in well-maintained neighborhoods but can <strong>decrease safety perceptions</strong> in poorly maintained areas. The key is not just greening, but <strong>combining trees with good maintenance</strong>.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Tree Effect on Safety by Maintenance Level</h4><canvas id='trees-safety-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>The relationship between trees and safety depends entirely on neighborhood maintenance. Trees increase safety in well-maintained areas but decrease it in poorly maintained ones.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Trees and Safety Paradox</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>2 Theories</div><div class='stat-label'>Competing Perspectives</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Austin, TX</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Street View</div><div class='stat-label'>Data Collection Method</div></div></div><p>Urban forestry has long faced a paradox: some research suggests trees increase safety through natural surveillance and community engagement, while other studies find trees create hiding spots and reduce visibility. This study resolves this paradox by examining the moderating role of neighborhood upkeep.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data Source:</strong> Google Street View images analyzed using computer vision for tree characteristics and maintenance indicators</li><li><strong>Perceived Safety:</strong> Systematic social observation (SSO) ratings from trained observers</li><li><strong>Tree Metrics:</strong> Canopy coverage, tree density, species diversity, and tree health</li><li><strong>Maintenance Indicators:</strong> Sidewalk condition, building upkeep, litter presence, graffiti</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Multi-level regression models with interaction terms</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Context-Dependent Effects:</strong> The relationship between trees and safety perception depends entirely on neighborhood maintenance levels</li><li><strong>Well-Maintained Areas:</strong> Trees significantly increase perceived safety (+15% improvement in safety scores)</li><li><strong>Poorly Maintained Areas:</strong> Trees decrease perceived safety (-8% reduction in safety scores)</li><li><strong>Tree Health Matters:</strong> Healthy, well-pruned trees have stronger positive effects than overgrown or dying trees</li><li><strong>Threshold Effect:</strong> Moderate tree coverage (30-50%) provides optimal safety benefits in maintained neighborhoods</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udf33 For Urban Forestry</h4><ul><li>Tree planting must be coupled with maintenance programs</li><li>Regular pruning and tree care essential for safety benefits</li><li>Focus on tree health, not just tree quantity</li><li>Consider maintenance capacity before large-scale planting</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfd8\ufe0f For Neighborhood Development</h4><ul><li>Invest in both greening and upkeep simultaneously</li><li>Address maintenance issues before adding trees</li><li>Engage communities in tree care programs</li><li>Design for long-term maintenance sustainability</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>This research challenges the simple narrative of \"trees = safety\" by revealing the <strong>critical moderating role of neighborhood upkeep</strong>. It demonstrates that environmental interventions must consider contextual factors to be effective, advancing more nuanced approaches to urban greening.</p></div>"
  },
  "streetscapes-servicescapes": {
    "title": "Streetscapes as Servicescapes: Walkability and Business Attractiveness",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can improving streetscape walkability make local businesses more attractive to customers? This study explores whether <strong>pedestrian-friendly street design</strong> acts as part of the overall <em>servicescape</em> that influences consumer behavior.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Walkable streetscapes significantly increase business attractiveness, with effects comparable to traditional servicescape elements like interior design. A <strong>one-unit increase in walkability</strong> corresponds to a <strong>12% increase</strong> in customer ratings.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Business Rating vs Street Walkability</h4><canvas id='servicescape-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Scatter plot showing positive correlation between streetscape walkability and business attractiveness ratings. Higher walkability consistently predicts better customer ratings.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Servicescape Theory Extended</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>1,200+</div><div class='stat-label'>Business Reviews Analyzed</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Atlanta</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Area</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>15 Types</div><div class='stat-label'>Business Categories</div></div></div><p>Servicescape theory traditionally focuses on interior environments (lighting, layout, d\u00e9cor). This research extends the concept to include <em>exterior streetscapes</em>, arguing that the pedestrian experience approaching a business is part of the overall service environment.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Walkability Assessment:</strong> Automated audit using Google Street View images with deep learning to measure sidewalk quality, street trees, building transparency, and pedestrian amenities</li><li><strong>Business Attractiveness:</strong> Yelp ratings and review sentiment analysis</li><li><strong>Controls:</strong> Business type, price level, parking availability, transit access, neighborhood characteristics</li><li><strong>Sample:</strong> Restaurants, cafes, retail shops, and service businesses</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Hierarchical models accounting for spatial clustering</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Significant Walkability Effect:</strong> Streetscape walkability independently predicts business attractiveness beyond traditional factors</li><li><strong>Stronger for Experience Businesses:</strong> Effects most pronounced for restaurants and cafes where customer experience is central</li><li><strong>Synergy with Traditional Servicescape:</strong> Good interior servicescape enhanced by good exterior streetscape (multiplicative effect)</li><li><strong>Pedestrian Infrastructure Matters:</strong> Presence of wide sidewalks, street furniture, and crossing amenities particularly important</li><li><strong>Return Visit Intentions:</strong> Walkable locations show higher rates of repeat customers based on review analysis</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfea For Business Owners</h4><ul><li>Consider streetscape quality in location decisions</li><li>Advocate for streetscape improvements near businesses</li><li>Invest in sidewalk-facing amenities (outdoor seating, displays)</li><li>Recognize walkability as competitive advantage</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udfd7\ufe0f For Urban Development</h4><ul><li>Streetscape improvements support local business vitality</li><li>Economic development and walkability are complementary</li><li>Pedestrian infrastructure has measurable ROI for commercial districts</li><li>Design streets with business attractiveness in mind</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>This research introduces <em>\"streetscape as servicescape\"</em> concept, expanding our understanding of how physical environments influence consumer behavior. It provides quantitative evidence that <strong>urban design is not just aesthetic but economically consequential</strong> for local businesses.</p></div>"
  },
  "environmental-equity-atlanta": {
    "title": "Environmental Equity: Urban Tree Canopy Patterns in Atlanta",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Are urban trees distributed equitably across different socioeconomic groups? This longitudinal study examines <strong>environmental justice</strong> in Atlanta's tree canopy distribution over two decades, revealing patterns of <em>green inequality</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Significant disparities exist in tree canopy distribution. <strong>Low-income and minority neighborhoods</strong> have 15-20% less tree coverage than affluent areas, and these gaps have <strong>widened over time</strong> despite overall canopy growth.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Tree Canopy Over Time by Income Level</h4><canvas id='equity-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Temporal analysis showing the growing disparity in tree canopy coverage. While high-income areas gained canopy, low-income areas saw minimal improvement, widening the equity gap.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context: Environmental Justice in Urban Forestry</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>1996-2014</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Period</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>18 years</div><div class='stat-label'>Temporal Analysis</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Metro Atlanta</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Region</div></div></div><p>Urban trees provide numerous benefits including cooling, air quality improvement, and health benefits. However, these benefits may be inequitably distributed. This study uses remote sensing data to track tree canopy changes across Atlanta's diverse neighborhoods over nearly two decades.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Tree Canopy Data:</strong> High-resolution aerial imagery and LiDAR analysis for 1996, 2008, and 2014</li><li><strong>Demographic Data:</strong> Census tract-level income, race/ethnicity, education, and housing characteristics</li><li><strong>Temporal Analysis:</strong> Change detection algorithms to track canopy gain and loss</li><li><strong>Statistical Methods:</strong> Spatial regression models accounting for neighborhood characteristics and urban development patterns</li><li><strong>Equity Metrics:</strong> Gini coefficients and concentration curves for distributional analysis</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Persistent Inequality:</strong> Tree canopy coverage positively correlates with income and education levels across all time periods</li><li><strong>Widening Gap:</strong> Despite 5% overall canopy increase, disparities between high and low-income areas grew from 12% to 18%</li><li><strong>Racial Disparities:</strong> Predominantly Black neighborhoods have 20% less canopy than predominantly white neighborhoods with similar income levels</li><li><strong>Development Patterns:</strong> New development in affluent areas preserves more trees than development in lower-income areas</li><li><strong>Loss and Gain Patterns:</strong> Low-income areas experience more tree loss and less tree planting/regrowth</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><div class='implications-grid'><div class='implication-box'><h4>\ud83c\udf32 For Urban Forestry Policy</h4><ul><li>Target tree planting programs to underserved neighborhoods</li><li>Provide resources for tree maintenance in low-income areas</li><li>Protect existing trees in vulnerable neighborhoods</li><li>Monitor equity metrics in urban forestry plans</li></ul></div><div class='implication-box'><h4>\u2696\ufe0f For Environmental Justice</h4><ul><li>Recognize tree canopy as environmental justice issue</li><li>Address structural factors behind green inequality</li><li>Ensure equitable distribution of climate adaptation resources</li><li>Engage communities in urban greening decisions</li></ul></div></div></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>This research provides <strong>longitudinal evidence</strong> of environmental inequity in urban tree distribution, moving beyond cross-sectional snapshots. It demonstrates that without intentional equity-focused interventions, <em>green inequality can worsen even as overall canopy increases</em>, informing more just urban forestry approaches.</p></div>"
  },
  "audio-sensors-pedestrian": {
    "title": "Audio-Based Sensors for Understanding Pedestrian Movement",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can <strong>audio sensors</strong> provide a novel, privacy-preserving way to understand pedestrian movement patterns? This study explores machine learning to detect pedestrian activity using <em>sound instead of visual data</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Audio sensors achieve <strong>85%+ accuracy</strong> in detecting pedestrians while preserving privacy - a <strong>scalable alternative to video surveillance</strong>.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Audio Sensor Detection Accuracy</h4><canvas id='audio-sensor-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Doughnut chart showing 85%+ accuracy in pedestrian detection using audio sensors, demonstrating viability as privacy-preserving alternative to video surveillance.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>85%+</div><div class='stat-label'>Detection Accuracy</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Privacy</div><div class='stat-label'>Preserving</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>ML Models</div><div class='stat-label'>Analysis Method</div></div></div><p>Audio sensors capture movement patterns without identifying individuals, addressing surveillance concerns in public spaces.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Audio Collection:</strong> Sensors in various urban settings</li><li><strong>Machine Learning:</strong> Deep learning to distinguish pedestrian sounds</li><li><strong>Validation:</strong> Compared with manual observation</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>High Accuracy:</strong> 85%+ detection rate</li><li><strong>Privacy Safe:</strong> Cannot identify individuals</li><li><strong>Cost-Effective:</strong> Cheaper than cameras</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Enables privacy-preserving pedestrian monitoring for urban planning and smart city applications.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Pioneers <strong>audio-based pedestrian sensing</strong> as ethical alternative to visual surveillance.</p></div>"
  },
  "asped-dataset": {
    "title": "ASPED: Audio Dataset for Pedestrian Detection",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How to create a <strong>comprehensive audio dataset</strong> for training ML models to detect pedestrians? ASPED is the first large-scale audio dataset for <em>pedestrian detection</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> ASPED provides <strong>100+ hours</strong> of labeled audio across diverse urban environments, enabling unprecedented pedestrian detection research.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>ASPED Dataset Distribution Across Urban Contexts</h4><canvas id='asped-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Distribution of 100+ hours of labeled audio data across different urban environment types, ensuring diversity for machine learning applications.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>100+ hrs</div><div class='stat-label'>Audio Data</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Multiple Cities</div><div class='stat-label'>Data Sources</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Open Source</div><div class='stat-label'>Public Access</div></div></div><p>No comprehensive audio dataset existed for pedestrian detection. ASPED fills this gap with carefully labeled, diverse samples.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Collection:</strong> Recorded in various urban settings</li><li><strong>Labeling:</strong> Rigorous annotation for pedestrian presence and activity</li><li><strong>Quality Control:</strong> Multi-rater validation (>90% agreement)</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Scale:</strong> 100+ hours of labeled data</li><li><strong>Diversity:</strong> Multiple cities, times, contexts</li><li><strong>Open Access:</strong> Publicly available for research</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Enables ML research in privacy-preserving urban sensing and smart city applications.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>First <strong>comprehensive public audio dataset</strong> for pedestrian detection research.</p></div>"
  },
  "tree-crime-austin": {
    "title": "Trees and Crime: Evidence from Austin",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How do <strong>tree characteristics</strong> (species, size, canopy) relate to street crime? This study examines <em>specific tree attributes</em> beyond simple presence.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Tree effects are <strong>highly nuanced</strong>. Large trees reduce some crimes but may increase others. <strong>Tree species and placement matter</strong> more than presence alone.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Effect of Large Trees on Different Crime Types</h4><canvas id='tree-crime-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Nuanced relationship between trees and crime. Large trees reduce property and daytime crime but show mixed effects on violent and nighttime crime, highlighting importance of tree characteristics.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Austin, TX</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>5 Years</div><div class='stat-label'>Data Period</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Multiple Types</div><div class='stat-label'>Crime Categories</div></div></div><p>Environmental criminology suggests vegetation affects crime through visibility. This study examines how tree characteristics influence different crime types.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Crime Data:</strong> Geocoded police records</li><li><strong>Tree Inventory:</strong> Species, size, canopy data</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Street-segment level regression</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Size Matters:</strong> Large trees reduce property crime</li><li><strong>Canopy Density:</strong> Dense canopy reduces daytime crime</li><li><strong>Species Variation:</strong> Deciduous vs evergreen show different patterns</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Strategic tree planting considering crime prevention alongside environmental benefits.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Demonstrates <strong>tree characteristics matter</strong> beyond simple presence for crime prevention.</p></div>"
  },
  "microscale-macroscale-streetscapes": {
    "title": "Integrating Microscale and Macroscale Urban Form",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can <strong>microscale streetscapes</strong> enhance benefits of <em>macroscale urban form</em>? This examines whether street-level design amplifies neighborhood walkability.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Microscale quality <strong>significantly moderates</strong> macroscale effects. Good macro form with poor streetscapes shows <strong>40% less benefit</strong>.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Walking Likelihood by Macro and Micro Design Quality</h4><canvas id='scale-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Synergistic effects of urban form scales. Good macroscale design with poor microscale quality loses 40% of potential benefit, demonstrating both scales are necessary.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Two Scales</div><div class='stat-label'>Analysis Levels</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Street View</div><div class='stat-label'>Microscale Data</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>GIS</div><div class='stat-label'>Macroscale Data</div></div></div><p>Urban design operates at multiple scales. This research integrates macro (density, connectivity) and micro (sidewalks, facades) levels.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Macroscale:</strong> Density, land use mix, connectivity</li><li><strong>Microscale:</strong> Automated audit of Street View images</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Interaction models testing moderating effects</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Synergistic Effects:</strong> Benefits multiply when both scales are good</li><li><strong>Threshold:</strong> Poor microscale limits macroscale benefits</li><li><strong>Sidewalks Critical:</strong> Quality most important micro factor</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Require microscale design quality, not just macroscale density in development policy.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p><strong>Scale integration is critical</strong> - neither alone is sufficient for walkability.</p></div>"
  },
  "automated-walkability-audit": {
    "title": "Automated Microscale Walkability Audit Method",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can we <strong>automate walkability audits</strong> using computer vision? This develops a method replacing costly manual audits with <em>scalable automated assessment</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Automated audit achieves <strong>90%+ agreement</strong> with expert audits while reducing costs by <strong>95%</strong> and enabling city-wide analysis.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Automated vs Manual Audit Agreement</h4><canvas id='automated-audit-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Radar chart comparing automated computer vision audit with expert manual audit across walkability dimensions. 90%+ agreement validates automated approach.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>90%+</div><div class='stat-label'>Agreement Rate</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>95%</div><div class='stat-label'>Cost Reduction</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>City-Scale</div><div class='stat-label'>Scalability</div></div></div><p>Traditional audits are expensive and not scalable. Computer vision offers automation using street-level imagery.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Expert manual audits</li><li><strong>Automation:</strong> Deep learning on Street View images</li><li><strong>Validation:</strong> Compared automated vs manual scores</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>High Validity:</strong> 90%+ agreement with manual audits</li><li><strong>Cost Efficient:</strong> 95% cost reduction</li><li><strong>Scalable:</strong> Entire cities in hours vs months</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Enables comprehensive walkability assessment at city scale for planning and research.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Validates <strong>automated walkability assessment</strong> as viable alternative to manual audits.</p></div>"
  },
  "walkability-walking-behaviors": {
    "title": "Walkability Factors and Walking Behaviors",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Which <strong>walkability factors</strong> most predict walking behavior? This uses big data to disentangle effects of <em>different environmental features</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> <strong>Street-level features</strong> predict walking <strong>as strongly as</strong> neighborhood factors, challenging macro-only approaches.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Variance in Walking Explained by Different Features</h4><canvas id='walkability-behavior-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Street-level features explain significant variance in walking behavior. Sidewalk quality alone accounts for 35% of variance, more than any neighborhood-scale factor.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>50,000+</div><div class='stat-label'>Street Segments</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Multiple Scales</div><div class='stat-label'>Features Analyzed</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Actual Behavior</div><div class='stat-label'>Outcome Measure</div></div></div><p>Most research focuses on neighborhood-scale features. Street view and computer vision enable street-level analysis at scale.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Behavior:</strong> Travel survey and pedestrian sensors</li><li><strong>Environment:</strong> Multi-scale features from GIS and Street View</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Multi-level models separating scale effects</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Street-Level Critical:</strong> Explains 35-40% of variance</li><li><strong>Sidewalks:</strong> Width and condition strongest predictor</li><li><strong>Trees:</strong> Independently predict walking frequency</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Invest in street-level improvements, not just neighborhood form for walkability.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p><strong>Street-level experience as important as neighborhood form</strong> for walking.</p></div>"
  },
  "align-navigation": {
    "title": "ALIGN: Smart Navigation for Individual Preferences",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>Can navigation apps <strong>personalize routes</strong> beyond distance/time? ALIGN generates routes meeting <em>customized environmental criteria</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> ALIGN generates routes optimized for preferences like <strong>shade, safety, accessibility</strong> with only <strong>8% distance penalty</strong>.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Route Trade-offs: Distance vs User Satisfaction</h4><canvas id='align-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>ALIGN generates routes optimized for environmental preferences with minimal distance penalty (avg 8%), while dramatically increasing user satisfaction compared to shortest path.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>8%</div><div class='stat-label'>Avg Distance Increase</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Multiple</div><div class='stat-label'>Preference Types</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Real-Time</div><div class='stat-label'>Route Generation</div></div></div><p>Current navigation ignores environmental quality. ALIGN incorporates spatially-resolved environmental data into routing.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Data:</strong> Street-level tree, lighting, crime, accessibility data</li><li><strong>Algorithm:</strong> Modified A* with environmental costs</li><li><strong>Validation:</strong> User testing with diverse populations</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Achieves Preferences:</strong> 90%+ user satisfaction</li><li><strong>Reasonable Trade-offs:</strong> Only 8% longer routes</li><li><strong>Accessibility:</strong> Valuable for wheelchair users, elderly</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Enables accessible navigation and human-centered smart mobility systems.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Pioneers <strong>environmentally-aware personalized navigation</strong> beyond time/distance.</p></div>"
  },
  "spatial-modelling-forecasting": {
    "title": "Spatial Modelling and Forecasting in Planning",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How can planners use <strong>spatial modeling</strong> for evidence-based planning? This synthesizes approaches for <em>understanding and predicting urban patterns</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> Integrating <strong>multiple spatial techniques</strong> - statistical, agent-based, machine learning - provides more robust insights than single approaches.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>Prediction Accuracy by Modeling Approach</h4><canvas id='spatial-model-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Integrated approaches combining multiple modeling techniques achieve highest prediction accuracy (85%), outperforming any single method.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Multiple Methods</div><div class='stat-label'>Modeling Approaches</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Decision Support</div><div class='stat-label'>Application Domain</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Evidence-Based</div><div class='stat-label'>Planning Paradigm</div></div></div><p>Urban planning increasingly relies on quantitative analysis. This chapter guides method selection and interpretation.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Survey:</strong> Comprehensive review of spatial modeling techniques</li><li><strong>Classification:</strong> Organized by purpose (description, prediction, prescription)</li><li><strong>Best Practices:</strong> Guidance on selection and validation</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Content</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Statistical Models:</strong> Regression approaches</li><li><strong>Spatial Interaction:</strong> Gravity and accessibility models</li><li><strong>Agent-Based:</strong> Simulating individual behaviors</li><li><strong>Machine Learning:</strong> Pattern recognition in complex data</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Adopt appropriate modeling tools and build organizational capacity for evidence-based planning.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Comprehensive guidance on <strong>spatial modeling for planning</strong> practice and education.</p></div>"
  },
  "pops-seoul": {
    "title": "Ground Floor Facilities and POPS in Seoul",
    "overview": "<div class='overview-section'><h3>Research Question</h3><p>How do <strong>ground floor facilities</strong> influence use of Privately Owned Public Spaces (POPS)? This examines whether nearby uses affect <em>public space activation</em>.</p></div><div class='overview-highlight'><p><strong>\ud83d\udca1 Key Finding:</strong> POPS near <strong>food/beverage and retail</strong> show 3-4x higher usage. Ground floor use is <strong>critical but overlooked</strong> for public space success.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Visual Analysis</h3><div class='chart-container'><h4>POPS Usage by Surrounding Ground Floor Uses</h4><canvas id='pops-chart'></canvas><p class='chart-caption'>Public space usage dramatically depends on surrounding ground floor uses. Food/beverage and mixed-use contexts generate 3-4x more activity than office-dominated areas.</p></div></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Study Context</h3><div class='context-stats'><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Seoul</div><div class='stat-label'>Study Location</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>50+ POPS</div><div class='stat-label'>Sample Size</div></div><div class='stat-box'><div class='stat-number'>Usage Patterns</div><div class='stat-label'>Outcome Measure</div></div></div><p>POPS provide public amenities in exchange for development bonuses, but many remain underused. This investigates surrounding uses.</p></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Methodology</h3><ul class='methodology-list'><li><strong>Observation:</strong> Systematic usage counts at different times</li><li><strong>Ground Floor Survey:</strong> Detailed inventory of uses within 50m</li><li><strong>Analysis:</strong> Regression predicting usage from land use composition</li></ul></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Key Results</h3><ol class='results-list'><li><strong>Food/Beverage Critical:</strong> 3-4x higher usage near cafes/restaurants</li><li><strong>Retail Important:</strong> Shops increase activation</li><li><strong>Office Insufficient:</strong> Office-only surroundings = low usage</li><li><strong>Mix Optimal:</strong> Diversity creates most vibrant spaces</li></ol></div><div class='overview-section'><h3>Implications</h3><p>Regulate ground floor uses surrounding POPS and coordinate public space with ground floor planning.</p></div><div class='overview-section innovation-box'><h3>\ud83c\udfaf Innovation</h3><p>Highlights <strong>critical role of surrounding ground floor uses</strong> in public space activation.</p></div>"
  }
}